{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>The hidden things.</b> The sense: one who worships idolatry in secret." "<b>to YHWH our Elohim</b> — the sense is that his judgment is in the hand of HaShem, and He will exact punishment from him. But if it was in the open, there is obligation upon us and upon our children to do as written in the Torah. Others say that \"the hidden things and the revealed things\" both belong to HaShem, and we have nothing but to do the words of the Torah. This interpretation has neither head nor foot, and what reason is there to mention such a thing in this place? And because it says, \"and He will cast them to another land,\" it says: if you return to HaShem, even though you are outside the Land, HaShem will restore your captivity. And according to the opinion of Rabbi Judah the first grammarian: כי יניח (for He will find rest), the sense being: He will find rest for the captives, and grant them compassion, and afterward He will turn and gather. n. 'The first grammarian' likely refers to Judah ibn Hayyuj; the Hebrew phrase מה טעם (\"what reason\") rendered literally; ואחר כן (\"and afterward\") retained despite seeming redundant with וישוב."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:25:30.320021118Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}